Conclusion
The Neo4j Customer 360 PoC successfully demonstrates graph-based relationship modeling and visualization.
Neo4j can scale from thousands to hundreds of thousands of nodes when supported by appropriate indexing, constraints, and query optimization.
Indexes should be applied to key lookup attributes (Customer ID, Account ID, Device ID, etc.) to ensure efficient node retrieval as data volume grows.
Graph traversal performance remains efficient because Neo4j follows stored relationships directly rather than performing multiple table joins.
For PoC and development environments, Local Neo4j is sufficient; for enterprise-scale production deployments, AuraDB provides managed scalability, monitoring, backup, and high availability.
Future enhancements can include large-scale performance testing, advanced graph analytics, and production deployment architecture validation.

The current PoC runs on a small dataset, but Neo4j can scale from thousands to millions of nodes. For 10K+ nodes, indexing customerId, accountId, deviceId, and transactionId becomes important to ensure Index Seek instead of full node scans. At 100K+ nodes, we should avoid loading full graphs and instead query relevant subgraphs with filtering and pagination. For deployment, Local Neo4j is suitable for PoC and development, while AuraDB is recommended for production because it provides managed infrastructure, auto-backups, monitoring, high availability, and easier scaling.

